      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{10}\\left(xy^{5}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{10}\\left(xy^{5}\\right) &=\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+\\log_{10}\\left(y^{5}\\right) \\\\ &=\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)=k$ and $\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=-5$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\log_{10}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{10}\\left(y\\right)=k+5\\cdot (-5)=k-25.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{10}\\left(xy^{5}\\right)=k-25.\n$$",
