      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{5}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{5}\\right) &=\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{2}\\left(y^{5}\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)=4$ and $\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=4$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)+5\\log_{2}\\left(y\\right)=2\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 4=28.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(x^{2}y^{5}\\right)=28.\n$$",
