      ":question/problem": "If $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=6$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=-1$, then $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}\\right)+\\log_{7}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=2\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=6$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=-1$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=2\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (-1)=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{7}\\left(x^{2}y^{3}\\right)=9.\n$$",
