      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{5}\\left(x^{5}y^{3}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{5}\\left(x^{5}y^{3}\\right) &=\\log_{5}\\left(x^{5}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=5\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)=-5$ and $\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)=-2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+3\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)=5\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot (-2)=-31.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{5}\\left(x^{5}y^{3}\\right)=-31.\n$$",
