      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_n(a)+\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- $\\log_n(a)-\\log_n(b)=\\log_n(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_n(c^m)=m\\log_n(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that $\\log_{7}\\left(x^{3}y^{4}\\right)$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\log_{7}\\left(x^{3}y^{4}\\right) &=\\log_{7}\\left(x^{3}\\right)+\\log_{7}\\left(y^{4}\\right) \\\\ &=3\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+4\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)=-4$ and $\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=-4$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\log_{7}\\left(x\\right)+4\\log_{7}\\left(y\\right)=3\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot (-4)=-28.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\log_{7}\\left(x^{3}y^{4}\\right)=-28.\n$$",
